Locator	Use Case
By.ID	When the element has a unique id.
By.NAME	When the element has a name attribute (e.g., form inputs).
By.CLASS_NAME	When the element has a specific class name.
By.TAG_NAME	When you want to find elements by their tag type (e.g., <button>).
By.CSS_SELECTOR	For complex queries using CSS selectors.
By.XPATH	For locating elements based on their structure or attributes.
By.LINK_TEXT	When you want to find a link by its exact visible text.
By.PARTIAL_LINK_TEXT	When you want to find a link by part of its visible text.

Best Practices:
Use By.ID or By.NAME whenever possible, as they are the fastest and most reliable locators.
Use By.CSS_SELECTOR or By.XPATH for more complex queries.
Avoid By.CLASS_NAME if the class name is not unique or if multiple elements share the same class.
Use By.LINK_TEXT or By.PARTIAL_LINK_TEXT only for links.

Wait:
    To wait between steps in Selenium tests, you can use:
    -Explicit waits (recommended):
    WebDriverWait(driver, 5).until(EC.presence_of_element_located((By.ID, "some-id")))
    -Implicit waits:
    driver.implicitly_wait(5) -> sets a global wait time for Selenium WebDriver
    - Sleep (not recommended for production):
    time.sleep(2)

driver.get()
    The method driver.get(url) in Selenium WebDriver navigates 
    the browser to the specified URL.
    It loads the web page at that address, just like typing the URL into
    the browser’s address bar and pressing Enter. 



## XPATH ###
    XPath (XML Path Language) is a query language for selecting nodes
    from XML or HTML documents. In Selenium, XPath is used to locate 
    elements on a web page, especially when IDs or classes are missing or dynamic.

    How to use XPath efficiently:

    Use specific attributes:
    //input[@placeholder="Nowe hasło"]

    Use text content:
    //button[text()="Zmień hasło"]

    Use contains for partial matches:
    //p[contains(text(), "Hasło zostało")]

    Use hierarchy for context:
    //form//input[@type="password"]

    Avoid overly generic or absolute paths 
    (like /html/body/...), as they are brittle.
    
    Example:
    element = driver.find_element(By.XPATH, "//div[contains(@class, 'card')][.//h5[text()='testowy']]//button[text()='Wybierz plan']")

        1. //div
        Searches the entire HTML document for any <div> element.

        2. [contains(@class, 'card')]
        Filters those <div> elements down to only the ones whose class attribute contains the word 'card' (e.g., class="card h-100 shadow...").

        3. [.//h5[text()='testowy']]
        Adds a second condition to the <div>: it must contain an <h5> heading inside it with the exact text 'testowy'.
        -> The leading dot (.) in .//h5 is crucial here—it forces XPath to check for the <h5> inside this specific card,
        rather than anywhere on the page.

        4. //button
        Once that specific "testowy" card container is located, it navigates inside it to find a <button> element.

        5. [test()='Wybierz plan'] 
        This part is attempting to match the text inside the button, but test() is an invalid XPath
        function and will throw an error in Selenium.

    /	Document root OR current element,	Direct child only (1 level down)
    //	Entire document OR current element,	Any descendant (any depth down)
    The dot (.) represents the currently selected node. It forces XPath to restrict its scope
    strictly to the element you are already inside.
    ./	Explicitly the current context node,	Direct child only (1 level down)
    .//	Explicitly the current context node,	Any descendant (any depth down) 

    Efficient XPath usage means writing selectors that are:
        As short as possible
        As specific as needed
        Resilient to layout or style changes
        Use browser dev tools (F12) to test your XPath 
        expressions before using them in code
            Inpect(Ctrl+Shift+I) -> Elements -> Ctrl+f -> pase XPATH



